An algorithm runs in $O(\log n)$ or Logarithmic Time when its execution time grows logarithmically relative to input size ($n$). At each step, the algorithm reduces the remaining search space by a constant fraction (typically by half).Key Indicators of $O(\log n)$Divide and Conquer: Halving the dataset size on every iteration ($n \rightarrow n/2 \rightarrow n/4 \dots \rightarrow 1$).Tree Traversals: Navigating balanced trees (Binary Search Tree, AVL Tree) from root to leaf.Logarithmic Loops: Loop counters that multiply or divide by a factor (e.g., i *= 2 or i /= 2).Operations BreakdownOperationComplexityReasonBinary Search$O(\log n)$Eliminates half of a sorted array on each comparison.BST Operations$O(\log n)$Search, insertion, and deletion in a balanced Binary Search Tree.Exponentials / Pow$O(\log n)$Binary Exponentiation ($x^n$ calculated via repeated squaring).C++ Code ImplementationC++#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

// 1. Iterative Binary Search - O(log n) Time | O(1) Space
int binarySearch(const std::vector<int>& sortedArr, int target) {
    int low = 0;
    int high = static_cast<int>(sortedArr.size()) - 1;

    while (low <= high) {
        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2; // Halves the search range

        if (sortedArr[mid] == target) return mid;
        if (sortedArr[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;  // Ignore left half
        else high = mid - 1;                         // Ignore right half
    }
    return -1; // Total iterations: log2(n)
}

// 2. Binary Exponentiation (x^n) - O(log n) Time | O(1) Space
long long powerLogarithmic(long long x, int n) {
    long long result = 1;
    while (n > 0) {
        if (n % 2 == 1) result *= x; // Multiply when exponent is odd
        x *= x;                      // Square the base
        n /= 2;                      // Halve the exponent
    }
    return result;
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> sortedData = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80};

    // Binary Search Example
    int index = binarySearch(sortedData, 60);
    std::cout << "Target found at index: " << index << "\n";

    // Power Calculation Example (2^10)
    std::cout << "2^10 = " << powerLogarithmic(2, 10) << "\n";

    return 0;
}
